  "capture": "Read from Meta’s public Marketing API documentation on 2026-10-11, without an account or credentials, by a documentation fetch that renders the page as text; three independent readings agreed on the 66 rows, the eleven currencies with offset 1 and the Gulf and Arab currencies listed or absent. The page shows no version or last-updated date, and no byte-exact page hash is claimed: sha256 seals the rows as recorded here. The page is on the provider documentation watch (tools/provider-lifecycle.ts), so a change asks for a new capture.",
  "offset_meaning": "Meta: each currency has an offset that sets how its subdivisions are counted. With offset 100 the smallest amount, 1, is a hundredth of the currency unit (USD cents); with offset 1 it is one whole unit (JPY).",
  "budget_unit_evidence": {
    "source_url": "https://developers.facebook.com/docs/marketing-api/bidding/overview/budgets",
    "captured_at": "2026-10-11",
    "statement": "Meta’s Budgets guide states that bid and budget amounts are set at the ad account currency’s minimum denomination (cents for USD); its example of a 20-dollar daily budget sends daily_budget=2000. The campaign reference states spend_cap as an integer of the currency subunit."
  },
  "differences_from_iso_4217": "Meta’s offsets are not ISO 4217 minor units: COP, CRC, HUF, IDR and TWD have offset 1 (ISO: 2 decimals) and BHD and JOD offset 100 (ISO: 3). KWD, OMR, IQD, LBP, MAD, TND and LYD are not listed, so a Meta cap for an account in one of them stays in native units.",
  "sha256_scope": "SHA-256 of JSON.stringify(currencies); src/budget-units.ts refuses the whole capture when it does not match.",
